WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.610
Hi! Why are some torches so bright? Why do other lights at home stay on when one bulb blows? Let's find out what affects the electric current.

2
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.242
With one battery in the circuit, the bulb has a certain brightness.

3
00:00:16.392 --> 00:00:23.370
If we add a second battery in series, joined end to end, the current becomes larger and the bulb glows brighter.

4
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.778
So the more batteries in series, the larger the current and the brighter the bulb.

5
00:00:29.928 --> 00:00:35.922
Now keep the batteries the same and change the bulbs. With one bulb, it is bright.

6
00:00:36.072 --> 00:00:41.226
If we put two bulbs on the same single path, they are connected in series.

7
00:00:41.376 --> 00:00:46.578
The more bulbs in series, the smaller the current, so each bulb is dimmer.

8
00:00:46.728 --> 00:00:53.586
And if one bulb in series blows, the circuit is broken, so all the bulbs go out.

9
00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:59.898
Another way is parallel: each bulb has its own path, connected straight to the battery.

10
00:01:00.048 --> 00:01:04.818
In parallel, each bulb is about as bright as one bulb on its own.

11
00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:10.554
If one bulb blows, the other is still on its own complete path, so it stays lit.

12
00:01:10.704 --> 00:01:18.594
The lights in our homes are connected in parallel. That's why the others stay on when one bulb blows.

13
00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:26.178
These experiments must be fair too. When testing the number of batteries, keep the number and type of bulbs the same.

14
00:01:26.328 --> 00:01:31.386
When testing the number of bulbs, keep the number and type of batteries the same.

15
00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:39.018
We look at how bright the bulbs are to compare the current: the brighter the bulb, the larger the current.

16
00:01:39.168 --> 00:01:46.386
Watch out! Some pupils think adding more bulbs in series makes each bulb brighter. That's wrong!

17
00:01:46.536 --> 00:01:52.362
The more bulbs in series, the smaller the current, so each bulb actually gets dimmer.

18
00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:58.746
To make the bulbs brighter, add batteries, or connect the bulbs in parallel.

19
00:01:58.896 --> 00:02:09.186
Your turn! Two bulbs are in series and one blows. Will the other stay lit? Pause and think.

20
00:02:09.336 --> 00:02:18.474
No. In series there is only one path, so when one blows, the circuit is broken. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:18.624 --> 00:02:28.722
Another one! There is one bulb in a circuit. How can you make it brighter? Pause and think.

22
00:02:28.872 --> 00:02:37.602
Add another battery in series. The current becomes larger, so the bulb is brighter. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:37.752 --> 00:02:46.770
Last one! Why are the lights at home connected in parallel? Pause and think.

24
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:54.834
Because if one bulb blows, the others stay on, and each light stays bright. Did you get it right?

25
00:02:54.984 --> 00:03:10.410
Remember: more batteries in series give a larger current and brighter bulbs. More bulbs in series give a smaller current and dimmer bulbs, and if one blows they all go out. Bulbs in parallel each have their own path, so the others stay lit.

26
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:15.882
Next time a bulb is changed, think about how it's connected!

